WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.834
-Our first guest tonight
is a talented actor

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:04.934
you know from films
such as "Rocketman,"

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:07.167
"Eddie The Eagle,"
and the "Kingsman" franchise.

00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:09.601
He executive produces
and stars in "Tetris,"

00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:11.901
which premieres globally
on Apple TV+ tomorrow.

00:00:11.901 --> 00:00:13.300
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.968
-Relax, Hank.
This game is swell.

00:00:15.968 --> 00:00:19.067
What's it called?
-"Tetris."

00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:21.801
-"Tetris."
I don't get it.

00:00:21.801 --> 00:00:24.300
It's a combination
of "tetra" --

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.200
Greek for "four."

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.567
All the game's pieces
are variants of four.

00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:30.567
And tennis.
-Tennis?

00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:32.868
-Tennis is supposedly
the Russian inventor.

00:00:32.868 --> 00:00:35.734
He, um --
He likes tennis.

00:00:35.734 --> 00:00:39.901
-Here, Hank. Try it.

00:00:39.901 --> 00:00:41.200
-Um...

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.367
♪♪

00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:49.033
[ Beeping ]

00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:51.934
♪♪

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:53.300
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.934
our friend Taron Egerton,
everybody.

00:00:54.934 --> 00:00:56.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:56.934 --> 00:01:07.000
♪♪

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:14.667
♪♪

00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:16.100
Welcome back.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.868
-Wow!
What a lovely welcome.

00:01:17.868 --> 00:01:19.334
Thank you so much. Thank you.
-They're a very lovely

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:21.300
group of people.
-Whoo!

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:22.701
-It's so nice to see you again.

00:01:22.701 --> 00:01:24.701
-It's great to see you.
-Last time you were here,

00:01:24.701 --> 00:01:26.868
we were talking about
your wonderful work

00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:28.367
as Elton John, in "Rocketman."
-Thank you, yep.

00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:30.400
-Obviously, you know,
a slightly...

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.300
[ Cheers and applause ]
The appropriate response.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.968
You know, obviously
it was taking on a lot,

00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:36.467
playing a real person.
-Yes.

00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:39.000
-You are also playing
a real person in this film.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.300
This is Henk Rogers, who was
integral in the "Tetris" story.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.734
This is a true story.
-Exactly.

00:01:43.734 --> 00:01:46.334
-Did you know him as well
as you knew Elton John?

00:01:46.334 --> 00:01:47.400
-No.
-Yeah.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.534
-No.
-So you weren't, like,

00:01:48.534 --> 00:01:50.901
a big Henk Rogers fan?

00:01:50.901 --> 00:01:53.968
-No, but I mean,
I am now, obviously.

00:01:53.968 --> 00:01:55.267
-And you got to meet
the real Henk.

00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:56.868
-I did. Yeah.
Well, we shot this movie

00:01:56.868 --> 00:01:59.934
during the first
sort of COVID phase,

00:01:59.934 --> 00:02:02.067
so we spoke over Zoom a lot.
He's a nice guy.

00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:05.701
He was a game publisher in 1989.
In the scene you just saw,

00:02:05.701 --> 00:02:08.501
he had got this sort of
sneak preview to "Tetris,"

00:02:08.501 --> 00:02:12.033
took it upon himself to go to
Soviet Russia on a tourist visa

00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:13.801
to try and procure
the rights to the game.

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:17.601
So he's this sort of, you know,
cowboy-ish, renegade --

00:02:17.601 --> 00:02:19.567
You know,
classic casting for me.

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:21.567
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:24.934
-So, he went to Russia to --
to get the game.

00:02:24.934 --> 00:02:27.167
-And now was he surprised

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:29.434
that his story was
being told on film?

00:02:29.434 --> 00:02:31.634
-No. I think they've known
for a while that it --

00:02:31.634 --> 00:02:34.868
'Cause the story gets crazy.
It's hard --

00:02:34.868 --> 00:02:36.767
I've been in New York
for a couple of days

00:02:36.767 --> 00:02:39.167
trying to, you know, explain it.
-Explain it.

00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:43.100
-And I'm rubbish.
I can't -- I can't do it.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.868
But it is wild.

00:02:44.868 --> 00:02:48.000
You know, the KGB got involved,
and there's jeopardy,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.000
and it's in Russia,
and it's gnarly, and it's cool.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.334
-It is not -- when you think
there's gonna be a movie

00:02:53.334 --> 00:02:55.567
about "Tetris," I was very
surprised when I found out

00:02:55.567 --> 00:02:57.267
that all these
very real details.

00:02:57.267 --> 00:02:58.400
-Yeah, I got there
on the first day,

00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.868
and I was dressed as a square,

00:02:59.868 --> 00:03:02.367
and everyone looked at me
like I don't know.

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:05.834
-You did -- I want to ask about
the mustache because I can't --

00:03:05.834 --> 00:03:10.133
I could not tell watching --
real or fake?

00:03:10.133 --> 00:03:11.501
-I can't believe
you're asking me that.

00:03:11.501 --> 00:03:12.667
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:14.501
I can't believe
you're asking me that.

00:03:14.501 --> 00:03:16.133
That's like ask--
-I can't tell

00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:17.868
why you're offended.
-Because it's like...

00:03:17.868 --> 00:03:19.868
[ Laughter ]
I'll tell you why.

00:03:19.868 --> 00:03:22.100
Because it's like
asking a lady her age.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.434
You must --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.434 --> 00:03:26.701
I don't know
why you're all laughing.

00:03:26.701 --> 00:03:29.634
You must never ask a gentleman

00:03:29.634 --> 00:03:31.200
if his mustache is real or fake.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.467
-You know what?
I apologize that I've stepped

00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:36.133
afoul of manners.
And you know what?

00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:38.167
We shall just leave it at that.
Let's leave that one there

00:03:38.167 --> 00:03:39.334
and move on with the interview.

00:03:39.334 --> 00:03:41.801
We can fix this in editing.
-Okay, great.

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:44.367
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:47.601
-This was --
You know, was this a game --

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:50.000
Because, again, this game was so
popular in the early '90s,

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.267
"Tetris"...
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:51.267 --> 00:03:53.100
-I mean, I know, also, I'm aware

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:56.300
that that's probably
right around when you were born.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:57.934
-Yes. Uh, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.934 --> 00:04:00.100
-So did you know much
about "Tetris"?

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.234
-No. No, I didn't.
I was actually born --

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:03.334
The movie ends --

00:04:03.334 --> 00:04:04.567
I'm gonna tell you
how the movie ends.

00:04:04.567 --> 00:04:06.567
That's left field, isn't it?

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:08.501
-The movie ends
when your mustache falls off.

00:04:08.501 --> 00:04:11.601
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.601 --> 00:04:14.234
And then it goes -- it's the
last piece of the "Tetris."

00:04:14.234 --> 00:04:15.567
[ Laughter ]

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:17.701
-Um, uh...

00:04:17.701 --> 00:04:20.534
So it's -- the movie's set
during the sort of last days

00:04:20.534 --> 00:04:23.467
of the Soviet Union.
And, obviously, you know,

00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:25.200
it kind of culminates
with the --

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.767
I really shouldn't do this.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:29.834
-Anyway, I was born
on the 10th of November, 1989,

00:04:29.834 --> 00:04:32.434
so I am an '80s baby
just by the skin of my teeth.

00:04:32.434 --> 00:04:35.167
-There you go.
-But I did play a little bit

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:36.734
when I was a kid.
I got a copy

00:04:36.734 --> 00:04:38.601
when I got my first Game Boy
when I was about 10.

00:04:38.601 --> 00:04:40.801
-What were the games
that you then played?

00:04:40.801 --> 00:04:42.300
Did you play video games?

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.033
-I was "Pokémon" --
obsessed with "Pokémon."

00:04:44.033 --> 00:04:45.334
-Okay. Gotcha.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:45.334 --> 00:04:48.968
-Obsessed.
Hey, thanks! That's cool!

00:04:48.968 --> 00:04:51.133
My, like --
My inner monologue was going,

00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:53.701
"You are the least cool man
on the planet."

00:04:53.701 --> 00:04:55.067
[ Laughter ]
But apparently,

00:04:55.067 --> 00:04:56.501
the "Seth Meyers" audience...
-No, no.

00:04:56.501 --> 00:04:57.701
I want to tell you
what happened there.

00:04:57.701 --> 00:04:59.367
They were applauding
for "Pokémon."

00:04:59.367 --> 00:05:01.033
They weren't applauding...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:02.200
They didn't think it was cool

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.300
that you were obsessed
with "Pokémon."

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.767
[ Laughs ]

00:05:05.767 --> 00:05:07.734
Don't look --
You're not even wearing a watch.

00:05:07.734 --> 00:05:09.634
[ Laughter ]

00:05:09.634 --> 00:05:11.634
All right. Is that --
Is that offensive that I asked

00:05:11.634 --> 00:05:13.033
if you're wearing a watch?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:13.033 --> 00:05:15.734
-Yeah. Anything that I have
to put on my body, you know?

00:05:15.734 --> 00:05:17.234
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.234 --> 00:05:18.634
No, I meant because, you know,

00:05:18.634 --> 00:05:19.901
"Pokémon" doesn't make me
feel very cool.

00:05:19.901 --> 00:05:21.968
But I was. I was obsessed
with that game as a kid.

00:05:21.968 --> 00:05:25.934
But I played "Tetris" sort of
when I got bored of "Pokémon,"

00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:27.467
which was quite rare.
You know,

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:29.334
almost like a sort of
palate cleanser.

00:05:29.334 --> 00:05:30.400
You know when you go
to a restaurant

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.000
and you have a bit of sorbet?
-Yeah.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.234
-"Tetris" is my sorbet.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:05:34.234 --> 00:05:35.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:36.701
I bet -- I'm confident

00:05:36.701 --> 00:05:38.934
you're the first person
to ever say that about "Tetris."

00:05:38.934 --> 00:05:40.534
-Let's get it --
Let's get it on a T-shirt.

00:05:40.534 --> 00:05:42.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:42.601 --> 00:05:44.868
-Your face, and it says,
"Tetris is my sorbet."

00:05:44.868 --> 00:05:46.667
-Exactly that.
-And I've just heard

00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:49.200
from the T-shirt store
that no one's buying it.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.968
-Really?
-We'll keep working on it.

00:05:50.968 --> 00:05:53.701
We'll keep -- Now, I've heard
that one of your dream roles

00:05:53.701 --> 00:05:55.467
is Sweeney Todd.
-Yes.

00:05:55.467 --> 00:05:57.934
-Which is currently on Broadway.
In previews right now.

00:05:57.934 --> 00:05:59.634
-Yes.
-Are you gonna have time?

00:05:59.634 --> 00:06:01.234
Are you here long enough
to go check it out?

00:06:01.234 --> 00:06:03.767
-No, but I just heard
that Maude Apatow is on the show

00:06:03.767 --> 00:06:05.567
and she's playing
Audrey, too.

00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:07.000
-Yes.
-"Little Shop of Horrors"

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:08.634
is, like, my favorite show.

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:09.901
-That's fantastic.

00:06:09.901 --> 00:06:12.300
-I had a -- I have a dream
about playing Seymour.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.701
I actually played --
When I was 17,

00:06:14.701 --> 00:06:17.200
I played that role
at the Aberystwyth Arts Centre,

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:19.067
which is the town I'm from,

00:06:19.067 --> 00:06:21.634
and it's one of
my real dream roles.

00:06:21.634 --> 00:06:23.067
♪ Suddenly Seymour ♪

00:06:23.067 --> 00:06:24.100
-There you go.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:25.167 --> 00:06:26.734
That's -- That was for you.
-Yeah.

00:06:26.734 --> 00:06:28.267
-That was for you.
-Thanks, guys.

00:06:28.267 --> 00:06:29.567
-That was -- Yeah.
[ Laughs ]

00:06:29.567 --> 00:06:31.200
-Yeah, I don't --
I'm judging you by your taste

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:32.834
because that was not my finest.

00:06:32.834 --> 00:06:34.267
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.267 --> 00:06:35.801
-And we do want to beg you.

00:06:35.801 --> 00:06:37.334
Just remember,
the next time you're on,

00:06:37.334 --> 00:06:39.667
please don't use your time here
to audition for Broadway stuff.

00:06:39.667 --> 00:06:41.000
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:42.801
Sorry. Sorry.

00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:45.167
-Do you -- When --
Did you do a lot of shows

00:06:45.167 --> 00:06:46.367
when you were 17?
Was that --

00:06:46.367 --> 00:06:47.934
-Yeah, that's when I --
So I started acting

00:06:47.934 --> 00:06:49.267
when I was about 15.

00:06:49.267 --> 00:06:51.734
I was quite late to it, really.

00:06:51.734 --> 00:06:53.968
And that was one of the ones
that I did in youth theater.

00:06:53.968 --> 00:06:56.334
It was an amazing thing
for me, acting,

00:06:56.334 --> 00:06:59.634
because I suppose,
like, prior to that,

00:06:59.634 --> 00:07:01.167
I was a bit --
one of those kids

00:07:01.167 --> 00:07:03.567
who hadn't quite found his --
his groove.

00:07:03.567 --> 00:07:05.334
-Sure.
-And when I started acting,

00:07:05.334 --> 00:07:07.868
I sort of felt like
I'd found my tribe.

00:07:07.868 --> 00:07:10.667
-Did you have those moments?
Because, you know, for me,

00:07:10.667 --> 00:07:12.534
it was -- it was maybe
doing improv comedy

00:07:12.534 --> 00:07:14.801
where you were on stage
in front of people your age,

00:07:14.801 --> 00:07:15.968
and it was going well...

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:17.300
'Cause I remember
the day I thought,

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:18.767
"Oh, I never want
to stop doing this."

00:07:18.767 --> 00:07:20.133
-Oh. I know!
I remember the moment.

00:07:20.133 --> 00:07:22.133
-Really?
Was it -- was it --

00:07:22.133 --> 00:07:23.667
What was it?

00:07:23.667 --> 00:07:25.234
-So this was when I was 15.

00:07:25.234 --> 00:07:27.601
And I -- And I -- And I
was in a production

00:07:27.601 --> 00:07:30.067
of a "Midsummer Night's Dream,"
which is a Shakespeare play.

00:07:30.067 --> 00:07:34.367
And I played an actor
within a show in the play.

00:07:34.367 --> 00:07:37.033
And he's kind of one of the
younger members of the company,

00:07:37.033 --> 00:07:40.834
and he's forced to play a woman,
and he's really upset about it.

00:07:40.834 --> 00:07:45.267
And as a sort of chubby,
awkward 15-year-old kid,

00:07:45.267 --> 00:07:46.834
I was really upset about it.

00:07:46.834 --> 00:07:51.567
And I was given a red,
like, tutu cocktail dress

00:07:51.567 --> 00:07:54.133
and flowers in my hair,
and a little thing.

00:07:54.133 --> 00:07:56.567
And I was -- I thought,
"Well, this is it.

00:07:56.567 --> 00:07:57.767
This is the end of the world."

00:07:57.767 --> 00:07:59.701
[ Laughter ]

00:07:59.701 --> 00:08:01.434
But I went out on stage,

00:08:01.434 --> 00:08:03.400
and the audience
fell about laughing.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:07.467
And I thought,
"Wow. This is it."

00:08:07.467 --> 00:08:10.133
-Yeah. It's like a drug.
-And that's why drag is okay.

00:08:10.133 --> 00:08:15.667
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:08:15.667 --> 00:08:17.300
-Well...

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:18.501
-Whoo!

00:08:18.501 --> 00:08:19.734
-I don't think
we're gonna top that.

00:08:19.734 --> 00:08:20.968
I think that's a perfect capper.

00:08:20.968 --> 00:08:22.167
Thank you so much
for being back.

00:08:22.167 --> 00:08:23.601
What a delight to have you.
-Thank you.

00:08:23.601 --> 00:08:25.934
Thank you.
-Taron Egerton, everybody.

00:08:25.934 --> 00:08:28.100
"Tetris" premieres
on Apple TV+ tomorrow.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:29.801
We'll be right back
with Maude Apatow.

